## Quality Gates

These run on every draft, in this order. Each one changes the draft. None of them send
anything: Slack, WhatsApp, email, Drive, and anything client-facing still wait for my
explicit approval.

### 1. Answer budget, before anything else

`.agent/skills/no-ai-slop/answer_budget.md` decides what belongs in the draft at all.

**Answer first. Stop at the answer. Reasoning is a reply to "why", not a part of every
reply.** No process narration, no closing recap, no explanation of a thing I did not ask
about. Ceilings that trigger a cut: chat reply to me 120 words, Slack or WhatsApp 80,
email 150, comment 60, chase note 40. The ceiling lifts only when I ask for depth.

**This applies to replies to me too.** I am a named human. Plain language, stop at the
answer, no justification of the answer I just read.

### 2. No AI slop, on anything a named human reads

`.agent/skills/no-ai-slop/SKILL.md` runs on every Slack message, DM, thread reply, email,
document comment, ticket comment, chase note, and reply draft, before the draft reaches
me. Edit in the main loop, self-check against `eval.md` in the same folder, then show me
the finished version. No before-and-after unless I ask.

Two rules from that skill are absolute:

- **The em-dash character is banned outright.** Reframe the sentence. Do not swap in a
  hyphen where the sentence reads better restructured.
- **Never claim an action that has not happened.** "I've sent it" and "I'm chasing him"
  are banned until the send actually ran. Say the true state plus a commitment with a time
  and a venue.

### 3. Simplified Technical English

`.agent/skills/no-ai-slop/ste.md` is on by default, on every communication including chat
replies to me. Instruction sentences at 20 words or fewer, other sentences at 25, one
instruction per sentence, active voice, present tense, articles kept, noun clusters of
three words maximum, one word per meaning, simplest exact word, no idiom or metaphor,
paragraphs of six sentences or fewer, warnings before the action.

The STE approved dictionary is not applied verbatim, because it has no vocabulary for
product work. Client and product terms stay. Quotes are exempt. Non-English drafts get the
structural rules only.

To turn STE off, delete this subsection. The rest of the gate keeps working.

### 4. Draft review

Then spawn the `draft-reviewer` subagent to verify the result: language, required
sections, tone, channel fit, em-dash, sourcing. PASS means show me. Issues mean fix first.
Running the reviewer before the edit wastes the pass.

### What the machine checks, and what it does not

`.claude/hooks/emdash_guard.py` warns on an em-dash written into a repo file.
`.claude/hooks/send_slop_guard.py` reads outbound text before a send: an em-dash blocks
the send, and banned words, slop phrases, an over-budget length, or an unrequested
rationale section raise a warning.

**A chat reply to me has no machine check.** No hook sees assistant text on its way to a
human. That half runs on these rules alone, so tell me when I go over and I will save the
correction.

